.TH BANDWIDTH\-LOGGER\-MONITOR 1 "2026-08-05" "bandwidth-logger 1.7.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bandwidth\-logger\-monitor \- record how much traffic the connection is carrying
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bandwidth\-logger\-monitor
.RB [ \-\-once | \-\-summary | \-\-prune ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bandwidth\-logger\-monitor
records throughput \- how much traffic your connection is actually carrying \-
by reading the byte counters the kernel maintains for each network interface.
.PP
This is a different measurement from a speed test. A speed test measures
.B capacity
by deliberately saturating the link. This measures
.B usage
without sending anything at all: it generates no traffic, consumes no
bandwidth, and needs no special privileges.
.PP
With no options it runs until stopped, sampling every two seconds and writing
one summary per minute containing the mean, the peak and the total bytes in
each direction. The peak matters: a ten-second burst at full line rate is
visible there even when the minute's mean is unremarkable.
.PP
This command is normally run by the
.B bandwidth\-logger\-monitor.service
user service, which is managed from the Throughput monitor section of the
application's Settings window. Ordinary use does not require running it by
hand.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-once
Take a single reading, print it, and exit. Useful for checking the counters
are readable.
.TP
.B \-\-summary
Print the most recent recorded minutes and exit. Records nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-prune
Delete samples older than the retention window and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-sample\-seconds " SECONDS"
Override the sampling interval. Clamped to between 1 and 60 seconds.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print machine-readable output.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Log at debug level.
.SH RETENTION
Throughput samples are kept for 30 days by default, adjustable in Settings.
.PP
Speed-test records are
.B never
pruned. That permanence is what the application exists to provide, and
nothing in this command touches them.
.SH CONCURRENT USAGE
While this monitor is running, every speed test additionally records how much
traffic was already in flight when it began. A test performed over a busy
connection measures only the capacity left over, so a scheduled test that
happens to fire during a large download records a low figure that looks like
a fault and is not. The stored context makes such a reading explicable rather
than mysterious.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/bandwidth\-logger/bandwidth\-logger.sqlite3
Throughput summaries are stored in the same database as the test history, in
the
.B throughput_samples
table.
.TP
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/bandwidth\-logger\-monitor.service
The user service, written when the monitor is switched on.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bandwidth-logger (1),
.BR bandwidth-logger-run (1)
